    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 092345\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n445 PM PDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* An incoming system will bring gusty winds and widespread \n  precipitation to the area Saturday through Tuesday. \n\n* Temperatures cool to below seasonal normals next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through the middle of next week.\n\nAll eyes are on tropical storm Rachel and an incoming trough from \nthe northwest, which will interact with each other to create a \nwidespread wind and precipitation event across the southwestern \nUnited States. Rachel will phase with a cutoff low as it tracks \nnortheast, then fall apart as it gets pulled by the upper level \ntrough into the southwestern United States. Remnant moisture from \nRachel will then fuel shower and thunderstorm activity across the\nregion. The heaviest rainfall will occur between late Saturday \nand Sunday night. Storm total precipitation amounts will generally\nbe between 1 and 3 inches, with the highest amounts in Lincoln, \nClark, Mohave, and eastern San Bernardino counties. Deep moisture \nand cold air aloft will allow for robust thunderstorm activity and\nenhanced rainfall rates, especially over high terrain. \nDevelopment of convective bands and training thunderstorms will \nalso bring localized areas of heavier rainfall. Flash flooding is \none of the main concerns and Flood Watches are in effect for most \nlocations. Another threat is wind. Remnant winds from the \ncirculation bring widespread sustained winds between 20 and 30 mph\nwith peak gusts between 45 and 55 mph out of the south. High \nresolution guidance suggests that even stronger winds may arrive \non Sunday as a dry slot develops over western Arizona and upper \nlevel winds mix down to the surface. Wind Advisories have been \nissued for much of the area as well. Precipitation will taper off \non Monday and Tuesday as the trough moves overhead and pushes the \nmoisture east. In addition, snow levels fall to less than 9000 \nfeet, opening the possibility for light snowfall over the Sierra, \nWhite Mountains, and Spring Mountains. \n\nThe trough will usher in cool air as heights aloft fall to around \n560 dm early next week. Surface temperatures will decrease 25 to 30 \ndegrees between today and Tuesday in response. Highs will be in the \nupper 60s to low 70s in Las Vegas and other valleys around the \nMojave Desert. Afterwards, temperatures will start to rebound \nWednesday onward as heights aloft start to increase.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Gusty \nsoutheast winds last into the evening before reducing and turning \nsouthwest. Expect light southwest winds through the night and into \nthe mid morning on Saturday before winds turn more southerly and \ngusts above 20 kts return. These winds increase into the mid \nafternoon with gusts peaking around 30 kts as showers begin to \napproach the valley. Showers will pass over the terminal into the \nevening with gusts decreasing slightly. These showers look to exit \ninto the night with gusts cutting off and winds reducing to around \n10 kts.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast \nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Mostly clear skies \ncontinue into Saturday afternoon. Breezy to gusty winds reduce \nthrough the evening, except for KDAG which keeps gusts into the \nnight before winds become light. Gusty south to southwest winds \narrive into Saturday afternoon with some chances for passing\nshowers.\n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Meltzer\nAVIATION...Murdock\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
